We tested whether a second application of native layer 23 could predict ordinary text without a router, task prompt or constrained outputs. The first pass supplied a full-vocabulary distribution. A second pass supplied a normalized query and keys to a 256-token continuous cache of earlier states and their observed successor tokens [16]. Three fitted scalars controlled temperature and interpolation. All predictions at position $t$ used only keys from positions strictly earlier than $t$. Training used 96 separate WikiText-2 training articles. The reported incremental checks ran the predictor on six actual prefixes without their future tokens.

Across 40 test articles (10,240 targets) and 120 existing retention passages (9,328 targets), the candidate scored a pooled NLL of 3.00688 versus 3.00592 for Base. It made one more correct top-1 prediction overall (8,320 versus 8,319). It gained 17 top-1 predictions on the original suite and lost 16 on WikiText. It improved likelihood on exactly 20 of 40 articles. All six incremental predictions matched the teacher-forced readout. The candidate used 11.66 s and 1.18 GiB peak versus 12.95 s and 1.82 GiB for Base. The result did not meet the required likelihood and top-1 gains on both corpora. The base-initialized student used 8-bit frozen weights outside the final layer. Both quantization and memory contribute to the system comparison.

